Policy - routing and route metrics
==================================
Will, 2026-11-21 10:40 CST: routing is configured so packets
route efficiently between nodes; WiFi is back-haul of last
resort, so his SSH terminals are not clogged.

metric: ethernet 100
metric: usb4 200
metric: wifi 600

Node-to-node traffic takes the best direct path: within a
row, over the USB4 interlink; between rows, over ethernet
through the row uplinks; never hairpinned through the WiFi
AP when a wired path exists. WiFi (metric 600) carries a
flow only when no wired path exists - including the paths
from Will's laptop (lilly) into the fleet, so bulk transfers
stop competing with his SSH sessions (see 1.1.1/1.1.1/3.3.1).

Known deviation: on systemd-networkd nodes the USB4
connected route carries the kernel-default metric rather
than 200 - each 10.54 /24 has exactly one path, so no route
selection is affected. Registered as D13.
Policy - proxies
================
Will, 2026-11-21 10:36 CST: each set of machines targets a
separate proxy, so we don't starve transfers.

Each row's machines target their own row's proxy host
(declared per row in chapter 2) on port 3129. No row's
pulls transit another row's proxy, and no proxy is reached
over WiFi once its row is wired: a row proxy host is a
wired uplink node by construction.

A row proxy must not inherit the legacy defect: the legacy
fleet proxy ssl-bumps with a CA whose certificates fail
verification fleet-wide (uncached PyPI fetches fail through
it; the ranch's crates.io path fails the same way). New
instances either splice without bumping, or bump with a CA
that verifies cleanly against the fleet's distributed CA -
tested with a real uncached fetch before the row is
repointed.

Legacy state, until each row is repointed: every node still
targets 192.168.86.22:3128/3129 - aletheia-core's WiFi DHCP
address. That single WiFi hop is the starvation mechanism
the census measured. Registered as D11.
Policy - lilly SSH priority
===========================
Will, 2026-11-21 10:40 CST: "SSH traffic to/from lilly
should have top priority."

lilly is Will's Lenovo laptop (tailnet 100.64.0.124, LAN
192.168.86.40). Beyond route preference (1.1.1/1.1.1/3.1.1),
SSH flows to and from lilly win contention on any shared
path - including when WiFi must carry them. Implementation
form: traffic shaping on node egress (tc priority bands and
DSCP marking for SSH to and from lilly's addresses), kept
simple, persistent, and enumerated wherever it is installed.

Verification bar: with a bulk transfer saturating a shared
path, an SSH session from lilly stays responsive - latency
measured before and after the shaping under the same load.

State: designed, not yet implemented on any node.
Registered as D12; the checker reports its absence per node
until it lands.
Enforcement - the checker
=========================
The checker is ~/workspace/ranch-network-fix/check_wiring.py.
It parses this file through libphext (PyPI phext, the Python
port of libphext; this file is cross-verified with SQ, which
is libphext-rs), extracts the machine-checked lines, and
probes the live fleet over SSH - sequentially, at human
pace, one session per node. For every declared element it
reports CONFORMANT or DIVERGENT with the observed value:

- node: a declared online node answers SSH and reports its
  own hostname. Declared dark, unlocated, and key-refused
  nodes are not probed; they are reported as their declared
  state, which is itself the audited fact.
- usb4 / xlink: the declared addresses are present on both
  ends, the peer answers a ping over the link, and a
  measured rate (tbw.py, 4 streams, 5 seconds) falls inside
  the declared expect-gbit range. A link observed on the
  wire but not declared here is reported as undeclared.
- uplink: the declared interface shows carrier, negotiates
  2500 Mb/s, and holds an address on the LAN
  (192.168.86.0/24).
- proxy: the row's proxy host listens on the declared port,
  and each online node's configured proxy (from
  /etc/environment and /etc/profile.d/ranch-proxy.sh) names
  its own row's proxy host at the declared port.
- metric: WiFi routes carry 600; USB4 routes carry 200 on
  NetworkManager nodes (the networkd deviation is D13).
- lilly priority (1.1.1/1.1.1/3.3.1): the tc state of each
  probed node is inspected for SSH prioritisation.

The checker reports; it does not reconfigure anything.
Remediation is a directed act. A divergence that matches a
registered entry in 1.1.1/1.1.1/4.1.1 is reported with its
D-number. An unregistered divergence is a new finding and
wants either a fix or a register entry - Will's call,
recorded in this file.
Known-divergence register
=========================
Every place reality currently departs from this declaration,
stated with its reason. An entry leaves the register only
when the departure is gone - fixed, or the declaration is
amended by Will to match a reality he accepts. The checker
tags its findings with these D-numbers when they match.

divergence: D1 keys node:DW - delta-wood is dark (tailscale idle, unprobed in the census). Row 2 links HV-DW (10.54.22) and DW-BW (10.54.23) are suspended until it is powered. Will's hands.
divergence: D2 keys node:BW - best-willow is dark. Row 2's right uplink and link DW-BW are suspended until it is powered; the census's 10th ethernet cable is reserved for it if Will powers it during his pass.
divergence: D3 keys node:GW - great-white refuses this VM's SSH key (publickey denied). Row 5 links JK-GW (10.54.52) and GW-QH (10.54.53) cannot be configured or verified until Will grants the key.
divergence: D4 keys node:TF - TF-16 is unlocated: drawn at (2,3) but no hostname or address exists in any record. Row 3 links PV-TF (10.54.31) and TF-LO (10.54.32) are suspended until the box is identified.
divergence: D5 keys node:VG - VG-16 is unlocated: drawn at (2,4). It fits victory-galleon by initials only (offline 223 days, unverified). Row 4 links AC-VG (10.54.41) and VG-WL (10.54.42) are suspended.
divergence: D6 keys node:AM - AM-16 is unlocated: drawn at (1,5). Row 5's left uplink and link AM-JK (10.54.51) are suspended; QH acts as row 5's proxy host and only reachable uplink meanwhile.
divergence: D7 keys usb4:KO-SC,usb4:KO-MB,usb4:MB-SC,usb4:YM-KO - moon-beam is bypassed: a single KO-SC jumper occupies 10.54.63.0/24 provisionally, where the drawn chain is YM-KO (10.54.61), KO-MB (10.54.62), MB-SC (10.54.63); MB has no thunderbolt_net loaded. Resolves when Will cables KO-MB and MB-SC (census ask-list A) and the jumper comes out; 10.54.63 then belongs to MB-SC.
divergence: D8 keys usb4:KO-SC-rate - the KO-SC jumper negotiates 40000 Mb/s (20 Gb/s lanes) but measured 8.8-9.1 Gbit/s with the two tools on those nodes (python meter, nc/dd; no iperf3 on either). An iperf3-grade retest is owed before the link itself is called slow. Related observation: every other built link measures ~11 Gbit/s against a 20000 Mb/s netdev - consistent with one 10 Gb/s lane carrying network traffic; inside Will's 10-40 Gbit expectation, at the low end.
divergence: D9 keys xlink:QH-LO - an off-diagram USB4 cable joins quacked-hill and lighthouse-omen (rows 5 and 3). It is physically sound, configured at 10.54.90.0/24, and not on Will's diagram. Keep it, or re-dress it to GW-QH when great-white returns: Will's call, undecided as of 2026-11-21.
divergence: D10 keys uplink:NK - ni-knight's ethernet cable lands somewhere that is not the LAN: carrier at 1 Gbps on a 2.5G NIC, no DHCP lease, gateway unreachable over it. NK's persistent wired-uplink profile is staged with autoconnect held off. Resolves with Will's trace and re-seat (census ask-list item 0).
divergence: D11 keys proxy:* - proxy repointing is not yet executed. Every node's proxy config still targets the legacy fleet proxy (aletheia-core at its WiFi DHCP address 192.168.86.22, ports 3128/3129), and only NK and AC run a squid at all. Resolves row by row as each row's proxy host is wired, its squid is verified (including the no-broken-ssl-bump rule in 1.1.1/1.1.1/3.2.1), and the row's nodes are repointed.
divergence: D12 keys policy:lilly - lilly SSH prioritisation (1.1.1/1.1.1/3.3.1) is designed (tc priority bands, DSCP marking on node egress) but implemented on no node yet; the under-load verification is likewise owed.
divergence: D13 keys metric:usb4-networkd - on the systemd-networkd nodes (EP, QH, LO) the USB4 connected route carries the kernel-default metric, not the declared 200. Each 10.54 /24 has exactly one path, so no route selection is affected. Deliberate deviation, recorded in USB4-STATE.md.
divergence: D14 keys diagram:colors - the wiring diagram's node colors (blue, red, gold) have no verified correlate: the census tested online status, Thunderbolt state, CPU batch (8845HS / 8945HS / Ryzen 7 255 all span multiple colors), RAM, and role. Unexplained; not guessed, and not machine-checked.
divergence: D15 keys uplink:* - every declared uplink except NK's is uncabled as of the census: no carrier (scroll-central explicit NO-CARRIER; orchid-flow and plain-view carrier 0 after bring-up; the rest down). Will's wiring pass (census ask-list items 1-10, inside his 10 cables) resolves them one by one.
divergence: D16 keys usb4:OF-HV,usb4:LO-FF,usb4:WL-RM - three links whose endpoints are both online are not yet cabled: OF-HV (10.54.21, census ask-list B), LO-FF (10.54.33), and WL-RM (10.54.43, census ask-list C). The census found zero live USB4 links among online members of rows 2-5. The links suspended by dark, unlocated, or key-refused endpoints are registered separately (D1-D6); these three wait only on cables, in Will's wiring pass or a follow-on.
External and inbound links - reserved
=====================================
Will, 2026-11-21: "wiring.phext will eventually include
others linking to us." This chapter is the prepared place
for links between the ranch and parties outside it. It is
empty today by design: adding a party later is an
inscription into this chapter, not a restructure of the
file.

Layout: this scroll (1.1.1/1.1.1/5.1.1) is the chapter's
register and form. Each external party takes the next free
section of chapter 5 - section 2, then 3, and so on - one
section per party, its first scroll carrying the party's
entries in the declared form below.

Declared form for an external link entry:

entry: <party> link <their endpoint> -> <our endpoint> medium <medium> authority <name, date> state <planned|active|suspended>

- who: the party's name, and a contact or handle where one
  is on record.
- what connects to what: their endpoint and ours, each
  named concretely (a host, a service, an address) - never
  just "the internet" on our side.
- medium: what carries the link - tailnet membership, a
  tunnel, a public endpoint, a physical cable.
- authority: whose authority the link exists under, and
  when it was granted - Will, unless he delegates in
  writing in this file.
- state: planned, active, or suspended. A suspended link
  keeps its entry and gains a reason, the way a divergence
  does.

Filed entries: none, as of 2026-11-21.
